# tauorm.async_session — the async story for session.tr's Session[T, C,
# RS] I/O methods (`get`, `commit`). See async_engine.tr's header comment
# for the full account of the three real tauraroc bugs `await
# generic_fn[T](...)` hit while landing this -- all three now fixed at the
# compiler level (missing monomorphization-trigger, an emission-order bug,
# and a wrong-return-type-inference bug in generic calls with an explicit
# type arg, the last one root-caused in a later session). A generic
# `session_get[T,C,RS](...)` helper is viable now; the concrete-per-entity
# pattern below was kept anyway since nothing required changing it.
#
# The proven-reliable pattern (confirmed directly, see
# tests/test_async_sqlite.tr) is a plain, CONCRETE (non-generic) `async
# def` per concrete (T, C, RS) combination you actually use -- copy this
# shape (it's ~10 lines) for each entity/dialect pair:
#
#   async def get_user(sess: Session[User, SqliteConnection, SqliteResultSet], id: int) throws DbError -> User:
#       mut r: Result[User, DbError] = sess.get(id)
#       if r.is_err:
#           raise(DbError.init(r.err.code, r.err.message))
#       return r.ok
#
#   async def commit_users(sess: Session[User, SqliteConnection, SqliteResultSet]) throws DbError -> bool:
#       mut r: Result[bool, DbError] = sess.commit()
#       if r.is_err:
#           raise(DbError.init(r.err.code, r.err.message))
#       return r.ok
#
# `await get_user(sess, 1)` / `await commit_users(sess)` then get the
# OS-thread hop (see async_engine.tr's header comment for what that does
# and doesn't mean) safely. `add`/`mark_dirty`/`delete`/`rollback` don't
# need an async counterpart at all -- they're pure in-memory bookkeeping
# (pending-object lists), not I/O; call them directly on a Session the
# same as in the sync case, async or not.
#
# A generic version of both functions above is now a viable, API-
# compatible upgrade (the underlying compiler gap is fixed) -- not
# attempted here since nothing required it.
